/P 525 0 R
/ActualText (Massive Open Online Courses \(MOOCs\) have expanded educational access, but students often struggle to choose the right courses, especially when exploring new fields. To address this, course recommender systems have been developed, but they face challenges like the "cold start" problem. Recent advancements suggest integrating Large Language Models \(LLMs\) into these systems to enhance personalization. Techniques like Retrieval-Augmented Generation \(RAG\) further improve LLMs by using external knowledge to provide contextually relevant recommendations. However, there remains a gap in research specifically addressing the use of LLMs in course recommendations.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
184 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 526 0 R
/ActualText ( Paul Mingzheng TANGa, Kenji Kah Hoe LEONGa, Nowshad SHAIKa and Hoong Chuin LAUa,* aSchool of Computing and Information Systems, Singapore Management Universit)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
185 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 527 0 R
/ActualText (This paper explores the use of Large Language Models \(LLMs\) for automating the conversion of static to dynamic scheduling models. Traditional static scheduling models require optimization experts and often become outdated, necessitating dynamic updates to handle environmental disturbances. The authors propose a Retrieval-Augmented Generation \(RAG\) based LLM framework, named RAGDYS, to automate this conversion process, allowing end-users to generate revised schedules through natural language prompts. This approach aims to minimize the need for technical expertise and computational resources, aligning with recent trends in applying LLMs to constraint generation in combinatorial optimization.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
186 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 528 0 R
/ActualText (Paper)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
