188 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 530 0 R
/ActualText (Wang, C., Long, Q., Meng, X., Cai, X., Wu, C., Meng, Z., Wang, X. and Zhou, Y., 2024. BioRAG: A RAG-LLM Framework for Biological Question Reasoning. arXiv preprint arXiv:2408.01107.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
189 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 531 0 R
/ActualText (BIORAG is a new Retrieval-Augmented Generation framework for biological question reasoning. It uses Large Language Models, a vast scientific paper corpus, domain-specific embedding, and a knowledge hierarchy to enhance retrieval. BIORAG employs iterative retrieval with search engines for current information and outperforms existing models in life science question-answering tasks. It combines internal and external sources with adaptive knowledge selection.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
190 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 532 0 R
/ActualText <>
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
191 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 533 0 R
/ActualText (This paper introduces Multi-Head RAG \(MRAG\), a novel Retrieval Augmented Generation framework designed to address multi-aspect queries in Large Language Models. MRAG leverages activations from the Transformer's multi-head attention layer instead of the decoder layer for document retrieval. This approach allows for capturing different aspects of data and queries, improving retrieval accuracy for complex, multi-faceted questions. The authors provide an evaluation methodology, metrics, and both synthetic and real-world datasets to demonstrate MRAG's effectiveness. Results show up to 20% improvement in relevance over standard RAG baselines. MRAG can be integrated with existing RAG frameworks and benchmarking tools. The paper contributes to addressing the challenge of retrieving diverse, relevant information for complex queries in RAG systems.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
